#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184024 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c023b634-e106-5fbc-a8ff-197f3aa4869d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the General Term"}] :question/id #uuid "f6c910a1-cd2c-5d9a-ad67-974aaba4a5b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-647" :question/problem "Does the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac {2 \\cdot (-3) ^ n}{5 ^ {n + 1}}$ for $n\\geq 1$ converge or diverge?\nIf it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <           1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form $a_n = a \\cdot r^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-3)^{n}}{{5}^{n+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-3)^{n}}{5\\cdot {5}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{(-3)^{n}}{{5}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\dfrac{3}{5} .$ \n\nSince $|r| < 1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184025 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126485fb-1b7f-5602-bc8d-05908ac6f37c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges"}] :question/id #uuid "77fc1164-55b1-5f37-a5af-02098da2a2ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-649" :question/problem "Consider the geometric sequence \n\n$$\na_n =\\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\right)^{n}, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is a positive real number. For which values of $x$ is the sequence convergent?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <                      1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r = \\dfrac{16}{x^2}.$ \nSince $x^2$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0 \\lt r\\leq 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0 \\lt\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\leq 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x^2 \\geq 16.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx \\geq 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184078 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges: Rearrangement Required"}] :question/id #uuid "ff1b9ef5-e99a-5252-b6d2-8981f2f9938f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9990" :question/problem "Consider the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac{x^{2n+1}}{x^{n-2}},$ where $x$ is a real positive number and $n\\geq 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <              1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form $a_n = a \\cdot r^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{x}^{n-2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2n+1-n+2} \\\\  & ={x}^{n+3} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r = x.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0 \\lt  r \\leq 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0 \\lt  x \\leq 1 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c023b634-e106-5fbc-a8ff-197f3aa4869d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the General Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441c9f37-3dd0-5851-ab77-2c242c104a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293279} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9685ab-4197-57b4-baac-df3712dcf51e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293280} {:answer/id #uuid "f4455a4c-9d99-51ea-821b-8aed170bffec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293281} {:answer/id #uuid "659252f2-417f-5652-a3f6-cbd6b0013357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293282} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfd0f15-ec01-5604-acd5-f53c2148a88c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "441c9f37-3dd0-5851-ab77-2c242c104a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293279} :answer-field/id #uuid "15320343-ff19-56b8-82e3-e715cf1fd21d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab0b47c0-5a92-5c1c-aef7-bc71e6c1d794" :question/math-academy-id "q-11193" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}}{{10}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}}{{10}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\cdot {(-3)}^{n}}{{10}^{n}} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{{(-3)}^{n}}{{10}^{n}} \\\\  & =-3{(-\\frac{3}{10})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\frac{3}{10}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges: Rearrangement Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "187d3e63-b629-50df-8c9f-8c5c6b4e14fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293286} {:answer/id #uuid "02689765-5087-5434-b9cd-898e90fe2759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293287} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d21f04-20c5-5e88-898a-07eecd9e3fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293288} {:answer/id #uuid "85aadba4-72e1-562a-a0a9-f3856aaf1d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293289} {:answer/id #uuid "28b067f0-d15a-543e-adeb-c4d5e34f88b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02689765-5087-5434-b9cd-898e90fe2759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293287} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb9f02f3-5a5b-5422-b093-4ec199edaa9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f05025b3-6a74-5e0a-8d1e-572323e114f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-123344" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{4}^{n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2n}\\cdot x}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & =x\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}$ is also positive.  So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTaking into account that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c023b634-e106-5fbc-a8ff-197f3aa4869d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the General Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c328c5d9-5de3-56f7-8432-3522255852b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293293} {:answer/id #uuid "cb839aae-cf18-5770-b35c-6416184695ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293294} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9a9242-f8e8-53e7-96cb-8b439ba26f58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293295} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6a6e13-33e5-56de-9163-fefba46e6a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $-\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293296} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7baccd-a8ee-5cef-8dd5-e7737bd742dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c328c5d9-5de3-56f7-8432-3522255852b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293293} :answer-field/id #uuid "643857a5-9540-59d0-a43f-59fcc5c1d814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c5b5131-1801-5dfb-aff8-f50ca940df15" :question/math-academy-id "q-141310" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{3{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}} \\\\  & =3{(-\\frac{4}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c023b634-e106-5fbc-a8ff-197f3aa4869d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the General Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95d05d2b-4821-5778-909b-12c49be5b108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293300} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7df908-0c70-5067-a015-f6c4c2f984c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{7}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293301} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d2da61-c712-59f6-af47-a4de37ba67bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293302} {:answer/id #uuid "558587b2-ae21-5e9d-8727-575843c06410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $-\\frac{7}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293303} {:answer/id #uuid "9620e259-4264-58a2-af55-5aa1c421e8dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9620e259-4264-58a2-af55-5aa1c421e8dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293304} :answer-field/id #uuid "9195b4d6-c546-5cb8-a824-f3dd7f368a55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fe74e91-2d2c-5519-91ea-b2c0bddf3ecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-141312" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{4{(-7)}^{n}}{{2}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{4{(-7)}^{n}}{{2}^{n}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{{(-7)}^{n}}{{2}^{n}} \\\\  & =4{(-\\frac{7}{2})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\frac{7}{2}.$ \n\nSince $|r|>1,$ the sequence diverges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c023b634-e106-5fbc-a8ff-197f3aa4869d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the General Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3b69a01-4ebc-54c8-bc14-99f4f4c13e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293307} {:answer/id #uuid "d02d5383-2234-5401-82fa-4fa911d7631d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293308} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2eda53-abcf-5b0d-8347-0e5d8694df17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{11}{9}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293309} {:answer/id #uuid "e9854a4a-6ac4-58d5-a2f1-e31269baf73f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293310} {:answer/id #uuid "798bd047-2738-51f4-9fee-e5d59362a37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "798bd047-2738-51f4-9fee-e5d59362a37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293311} :answer-field/id #uuid "b582b2f0-c33c-5928-afbf-9c10f50f5eae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73837bab-c14a-5484-97cb-d95e1e5a0855" :question/math-academy-id "q-141314" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{8\\cdot {11}^{n}}{{9}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{8\\cdot {11}^{n}}{{9}^{n}} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{11}^{n}}{{9}^{n}} \\\\  & =8{(\\frac{11}{9})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=\\frac{11}{9}.$ \n\nSince $|r|>1,$ the sequence diverges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126485fb-1b7f-5602-bc8d-05908ac6f37c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0210bad6-e105-5720-9621-9b1a5ad59ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293314} {:answer/id #uuid "9374f8de-2ad5-5e03-9cda-b3724141b75f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293315} {:answer/id #uuid "466b0c36-a35a-5ce7-b549-d0dac3a6e0b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293316} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6c8149-9b96-539e-8c70-11c08d12ba9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293317} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9a87b8-20d0-55dc-90fc-5abf84a2e501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9374f8de-2ad5-5e03-9cda-b3724141b75f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293315} :answer-field/id #uuid "1749026b-dec2-5335-90eb-4955af40336a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "873c6085-4833-5124-b550-d113c8cac7d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-141318" :question/problem "Consider the geometric sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{x}{(2x)}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=2x.$ \nSince $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<2x\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126485fb-1b7f-5602-bc8d-05908ac6f37c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72339277-a4ca-5cf4-9a53-9bbb934d0e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293321} {:answer/id #uuid "f3071869-b2f6-5a56-8be8-7b60f0fe4139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293322} {:answer/id #uuid "a566fd21-f4f0-5952-9dea-d1071f8e28b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293323} {:answer/id #uuid "47b01622-9184-5fa3-b41e-d61bd69a85c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293324} {:answer/id #uuid "ee670c7b-0721-5856-879d-022ed9bf236c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47b01622-9184-5fa3-b41e-d61bd69a85c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293324} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f00b020-81e5-5940-bfc3-9a677aa0424b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ecf569a-17ef-54e4-9a64-9ec3bdecb9da" :question/math-academy-id "q-141323" :question/problem "Consider the geometric sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{x}{4}{(\\frac{8}{x})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{8}{x}.$ \nSince $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{8}{x}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge 8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126485fb-1b7f-5602-bc8d-05908ac6f37c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e290f5ea-6bef-5cdd-bccc-9e5d6b8ca35f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293328} {:answer/id #uuid "8e531f7b-f637-550b-922a-4b3dab14981c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293329} {:answer/id #uuid "9755169a-295f-55b1-bb44-4ced86139c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293330} {:answer/id #uuid "00d2b546-079e-5e0a-94b3-755fed682f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293331} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8e6cca-26fe-5dd6-ab39-6ba2243b5dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9755169a-295f-55b1-bb44-4ced86139c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293330} :answer-field/id #uuid "170c7709-3aca-5d51-a523-b62ef8ff639f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8be74ccf-bbd3-56f1-a2ff-c77c6c122f36" :question/math-academy-id "q-141332" :question/problem "Consider the geometric sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{x}{2}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.$ \nSince ${x}^{2}$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx\\ge \\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges: Rearrangement Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d84806e1-564d-5919-aaf4-135ae3357756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293335} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9bcbd0-2e1b-554e-af72-46b48bd0521c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293336} {:answer/id #uuid "711c5c2d-64ad-5ec4-ac8c-7f03e42142e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293337} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8e88a6-3dd8-5d92-93d2-bf1b0e7d0112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293338} {:answer/id #uuid "ef467f4e-409b-517a-aeec-b3fa016a3e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e9bcbd0-2e1b-554e-af72-46b48bd0521c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293336} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef9f971-5077-59e8-815a-0e5b8718a9d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5a00789-960c-5600-b89b-62bbd0168b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141336" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{16}^{n+1}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{16}^{n+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{16\\cdot {16}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{16}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r$ is also positive.  So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le 16.\n$$\n\nTaking into account that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126485fb-1b7f-5602-bc8d-05908ac6f37c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "995c8408-eabb-5584-8c05-afb7cfc4d9fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "995c8408-eabb-5584-8c05-afb7cfc4d9fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293342} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb6f2b7c-47fc-59a2-abdc-f088694d4b56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293341} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d395a9f6-0343-5086-b068-3f378b188f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d395a9f6-0343-5086-b068-3f378b188f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293344} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8287ae1-9e32-5922-86f0-37098b44100e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293343} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e9d525a-7061-59a0-be3a-e49883f67195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293346}] :answer-field/correct {:answ