1\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 4.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx\\ge 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges: Rearrangement Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2741cff5-299e-52df-85d9-7c5cc30c09d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293354} {:answer/id #uuid "61918bf9-c6bb-51a1-b6c9-46f42e7f06d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2741cff5-299e-52df-85d9-7c5cc30c09d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293354} :answer-field/id #uuid "37c79175-7c41-5789-a713-e1c4d23f82ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293353} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51928584-76c6-552c-9593-29a9eeb41693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51928584-76c6-552c-9593-29a9eeb41693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293357} :answer-field/id #uuid "099d1a1b-b0db-583a-a75d-9fa2d2ffc314" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbb4cb0e-6156-5433-b10c-c0559539044a" :question/math-academy-id "q-351624" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{4-n}}{{x}^{5n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ Find the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges.\n\n$x$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{4-n}}{{x}^{5n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{-n}}{{x}^{5n}} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{6n}} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.$ Since ${x}^{6}$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,{x}^{6}\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges: Rearrangement Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e76fa6e3-84b4-5fe1-b544-d83aaff0d617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e76fa6e3-84b4-5fe1-b544-d83aaff0d617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293360} :answer-field/id #uuid "df8ee542-5787-5f0c-aa1d-64abe31b048a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293359} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96fe9bdf-751c-5475-a4c1-20ac570cbf32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96fe9bdf-751c-5475-a4c1-20ac570cbf32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293362} :answer-field/id #uuid "e918285d-b1e6-59a6-804c-73ea49b5420d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293361} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89b5003b-d228-5664-b33b-fc76e3a0b964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89b5003b-d228-5664-b33b-fc76e3a0b964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293364} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe42bd9-1445-5ba6-a814-f64805c3ce4e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c54086d-c19a-5134-b16f-78dfd14ffbc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-351812" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{3n+2}}{{x}^{2n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ Find the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<x$\n\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{3n+2}}{{x}^{2n}} \\\\  & ={x}^{\\,3n+2-2n} \\\\  & ={x}^{\\,n+2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=x.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c023b634-e106-5fbc-a8ff-197f3aa4869d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the General Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3821cdf-ab7e-55b8-ba3f-f685ca40455c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293367} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb036f9-b547-5f88-9c5f-5fe5d5153cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293368} {:answer/id #uuid "860e055d-1c4d-5143-8de0-185ecb97e696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{2}{25}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293369} {:answer/id #uuid "1e5c1fcb-334e-59c9-83fe-39d8bd325b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{2}{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293370} {:answer/id #uuid "670faddf-4300-5f7b-a824-5358783739dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3821cdf-ab7e-55b8-ba3f-f685ca40455c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293367} :answer-field/id #uuid "da157a79-ecb0-57d0-9cba-04f0c140fc15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e5a6119-0a8a-5865-a2e3-cc07719b0415" :question/math-academy-id "q-49565" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{(2)}^{n}}{{5}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{3{(2)}^{n}}{{5}^{n}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}}{{5}^{n}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=\\frac{2}{5}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges: Rearrangement Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f47ee072-c12d-508a-98e0-716d0a8e52e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293374} {:answer/id #uuid "58d775e7-38de-5a1d-898b-33b5dab186c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293375} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4e8e39-e893-5354-b663-09ac379deecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293376} {:answer/id #uuid "a8537119-574a-5f8a-9df6-e74b7f9e6bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293377} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4fc8ea-769c-56d3-94e8-d87f2544c172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e4fc8ea-769c-56d3-94e8-d87f2544c172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293378} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b8db26-97d0-5544-b6e5-d7e0671f2706" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ccc82d1a-8b77-5855-b94b-af517ea6204a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74633" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{2}^{n+4}}{{x}^{n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{2}^{n+4}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{n}\\cdot {2}^{4}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =16{(\\frac{2}{x})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{2}{x}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge 2.\n$$"}]}
